## The `java.time` API in a nutshell

Since Java 8 the *new* date‑time library (`java.time`) replaced the old `java.util.Date`, `Calendar`, and `SimpleDateFormat`.  
It was designed to be:

| Feature | Old API | New API |
|---------|---------|---------|
| **Immutability** | `Date`/`Calendar` are mutable and not thread‑safe | All classes (`LocalDate`, `ZonedDateTime`, …) are immutable & thread‑safe |
| **Readability** | “magic” fields (`date.getMonth()`, `date.getYear()`) | Clearly named methods (`date.getMonthValue()`, `date.getYear()`) |
| **Time‑zone support** | Very clunky | Built‑in (`ZoneId`, `ZonedDateTime`, `OffsetDateTime`) |
| **ISO‑8601** | Not the default | Default format |
| **Calculations** | `Calendar.add()`, `Calendar.add(Calendar.DAY_OF_MONTH, 1)` | `plusDays()`, `minusWeeks()`, `Period`, `Duration` |
| **Formatting/Parsing** | `SimpleDateFormat` (not thread‑safe) | `DateTimeFormatter` (thread‑safe) |

The core idea:  
* **`LocalDate`** – a date (YYYY‑MM‑DD) with no time‑zone.  
* **`LocalTime`** – a time (HH:MM:SS.nnn) with no time‑zone.  
* **`LocalDateTime`** – a date + time (no zone).  
* **`OffsetDateTime`** – a date + time + offset from UTC (e.g. `+02:00`).  
* **`ZonedDateTime`** – a date + time + full time‑zone (`Europe/Paris`).  
* **`Instant`** – a moment on the global timeline (seconds + nanoseconds since epoch).  
* **`Period`** – a date‑based amount (days, weeks, months, years).  
* **`Duration`** – a time‑based amount (nanoseconds, seconds, minutes, etc.).  

---

## Simple example

```java
import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;

public class TimeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        /* 1️⃣  Current date & simple arithmetic */
        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();               // e.g. 2026-10-09
        LocalDate nextWeek = today.plusWeeks(1);         // +7 days
        System.out.println("Today:       " + today);
        System.out.println("Next week:   " + nextWeek);

        /* 2️⃣  Formatting a date */
        DateTimeFormatter fmt = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd MMM yyyy");
        String formatted = nextWeek.format(fmt);          // "16 Oct 2026"
        System.out.println("Formatted:   " + formatted);

        /* 3️⃣  Parsing a string back to a date */
        String input = "2023-12-31";
        LocalDate parsed = LocalDate.parse(input, DateTimeFormatter.ISO_DATE);
        System.out.println("Parsed:      " + parsed);      // 2023-12-31

        /* 4️⃣  Time‑zone aware datetime */
        ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.of(
                2023, 12, 31, 23, 59, 59, 0,
                ZoneId.of("America/New_York"));          // 2023-12-31T23:59:59-05:00[America/New_York]
        System.out.println("ZonedDateTime:" + zdt);

        /* 5️⃣  Convert to Instant (absolute point in time) */
        Instant instant = zdt.toInstant();               // 2024-01-01T04:59:59Z
        System.out.println("Instant:     " + instant);

        /* 6️⃣  Calculate a difference (days) */
        long daysBetween = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(today, nextWeek);
        System.out.println("Days between today & next week: " + daysBetween);

        /* 7️⃣  Duration (time‑based) */
        Duration dur = Duration.between(LocalTime.of(8, 0),
                                         LocalTime.of(17, 30));
        System.out.println("Workday length (minutes): " + dur.toMinutes());

        /* 8️⃣  Period (date‑based) */
        Period period = Period.between(parsed, nextWeek);
        System.out.println("Period (years, months, days): " + period);
    }
}
```

### What the program prints (illustrative)

```
Today:       2026-10-09
Next week:   2026-10-16
Formatted:   16 Oct 2026
Parsed:      2023-12-31
ZonedDateTime:2023-12-31T23:59:59-05:00[America/New_York]
Instant:     2024-01-01T04:59:59Z
Days between today & next week: 7
Workday length (minutes): 570
Period (years, months, days): P-2Y9M-8D   // (depends on the dates)
```

---

## Key take‑aways

| Topic | What to remember |
|-------|------------------|
| **Immutability** | All `java.time` objects are immutable – you never change the original instance. |
| **Thread safety** | Safe to share between threads. |
| **Default format** | ISO‑8601 (`yyyy-MM-dd` for dates, `HH:mm:ss` for times). |
| **Zones** | `ZonedDateTime` gives the full zone rules (DST, leap seconds). |
| **Instant** | The only class that can be used when you need a single number (epoch‑seconds). |
| **Formatting** | `DateTimeFormatter` is the replacement for `SimpleDateFormat`; it’s thread‑safe. |
| **Arithmetic** | `plusDays()`, `minusMonths()`, `plusWeeks()`, `plus(Period)` – all return new instances. |
| **Difference** | `ChronoUnit` or `Duration` / `Period` to compute gaps. |

With these concepts in hand, you can comfortably work with dates, times, time‑zones, and durations in modern Java. Happy coding!
